import{_ as t,o as a,c as i,a3 as n}from"./chunks/framework.BwzYn71E.js";const m=JSON.parse('{"title":"Modified Quantitative Calculator","description":"","frontmatter":{"translationSource":{"title":"魔改量化计算器","archivedAt":"2026-10-27","url":"https://docs.qq.com/aio/DTmluZ1diWlpQZldw?p=NdPCAZI5kpmdOZobXpuDCr","scope":"page"},"translationLang":"en","head":[["link",{"rel":"canonical","href":"https://kokobird28144.github.io/endfield-skydoc/modified-quantitative-calculator"}],["link",{"rel":"alternate","hreflang":"en","href":"https://kokobird28144.github.io/endfield-skydoc/modified-quantitative-calculator"}],["link",{"rel":"alternate","hreflang":"zh-CN","href":"https://kokobird28144.github.io/endfield-skydoc/zh/modified-quantitative-calculator"}],["link",{"rel":"alternate","hreflang":"x-default","href":"https://kokobird28144.github.io/endfield-skydoc/modified-quantitative-calculator"}]]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"modified-quantitative-calculator.md","filePath":"logistics/src/page-NdPCAZI5kpmdOZobXpuDCr.md"}'),o={name:"modified-quantitative-calculator.md"};function r(s,e,l,c,d,u){return a(),i("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n('<div class="logistics-content"><span class="logistics-source-anchor" id="魔改量化计算器" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="logistics-source-anchor" id="quantitative-calculator-improvements-and-implementation" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="logistics-source-anchor" id="量化计算器改进点与实现方案" aria-hidden="true"></span><h1 id="modified-quantitative-calculator" tabindex="-1">Modified Quantitative Calculator <a class="header-anchor" href="#modified-quantitative-calculator" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Modified Quantitative Calculator&quot;">​</a></h1><blockquote><p>Source page ID: <code>NdPCAZI5kpmdOZobXpuDCr</code> | <a href="https://docs.qq.com/aio/DTmluZ1diWlpQZldw?p=NdPCAZI5kpmdOZobXpuDCr" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open in Tencent Docs</a></p></blockquote><p><span id="source-block-pGtU40WJ0lS03XwNlupa3C"></span></p><span class="logistics-source-anchor" id="具体目标" aria-hidden="true"></span><h4 id="specific-goals" tabindex="-1">Specific Goals <a class="header-anchor" href="#specific-goals" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Specific Goals&quot;">​</a></h4><p>Standard calculator function: input material constraints, facility constraints, possible recipes and an optimization objective; output operating rates (facility counts) for each recipe, with an explanatory display.</p><p>Power balance: include power-generation recipes and facility consumption among the inputs, plus additional power demand. The solution should generate enough power while remaining optimal.</p><p>Restrict Sewage production: require final Sewage output to be 0.</p><p>Limit Forge of the Sky count: require fewer than a specified number of these facilities.</p><p>Over-capacity transfer: automatically calculate what is best to transfer beyond storage capacity.</p><p>Toggle constant activation: require integer numbers of activated facilities, allowing facilities to be activated but idle.</p><p>Wasted environmental capacity: for example, with only two Purification Units in an Inergen environment, efficiency is only 1/2 that of four, so the original recipe efficiency is no longer achieved.</p><p>Serial/parallel operation: calculate serial operation (alternating between products to reduce facility requirements) and parallel operation (a reaction tank producing different products simultaneously to reduce facility requirements). Further, serial mixed tanks have a utilization ceiling, and parallel operation needs a whitelist of permitted combinations.</p><p>Separate terminal-product chains in the display: perhaps let users click a terminal product to view its chain of inputs and intermediates, ignoring other chains or displaying them translucently.</p><p>Line partitioning: separate inputs used for different products to make the display more modular.</p><p>Recipe uploads: allow custom recipes.</p><p>Let users choose Solid-Gas/Fluid-Gas: this resembles line simplification. Specify that the input for a particular line comes only from a chosen production method, without restricting sources of the same material for other lines.</p><p>Newcomer rounding mode: round automatically, for example to multiples of 6 for activation materials and integer output rates per minute, without affecting normal solutions close to the maximum.</p><p>UI improvements: ? Does that belong here?</p><p>Explain the reasons: analyze why this solution is chosen.</p><p>Regarding production lines and inputs, these materials suit Wuling as inputs: Sewage, Clean Water, Precipitation Acid, Originium Ore, Ferrium Ore/Ferrium, Cuprium Ore/Cuprium, Xiranite, Heavy Xiranite (the Forge of the Sky can make it), a stable environment (do not ask why this is needed alongside Inergen), Xircon Effluent and Inergen. (jnk)</p><p><span id="source-block-ryMw8uNIDorsPZ7aHoogh6"></span></p><span class="logistics-source-anchor" id="抽象痛点与需求分析-抽象这一块" aria-hidden="true"></span><h4 id="abstract-pain-points-and-requirements-the-abstract-part" tabindex="-1">Abstract Pain Points and Requirements (the Abstract Part) <a class="header-anchor" href="#abstract-pain-points-and-requirements-the-abstract-part" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Abstract Pain Points and Requirements (the Abstract Part)&quot;">​</a></h4><details><summary>zmd is not that complex yet, but once there are more by-products and more recipes for the same product, it feels like it will become hard to use and take ages to adjust. Ideally, users would assemble things themselves. I think the issue is by-products rather than mixed production: they clutter the result, even though I do not want to focus on them. Multiple recipes for the same product also make things messy. (☢caution☢caution☢)</summary><p>Would it help if, in the current web calculator, clicking a terminal product highlighted or even separated its intermediates and inputs, setting by-products aside?</p></details><details><summary>Could our own calculator add conditions for when mixed belts save power? Then it could calculate everything at once, instead of requiring so much manual work. (c)</summary><p>This probably means mixed reaction tanks. First we need to establish which combinations work. Xircon production in an Expanded Crucible involves eight items in eight slots, but still blocks without special design. Some differences between regular and Expanded Crucibles also remain unclear.</p></details><details><summary>I think quantitative calculators have a lot of room to improve. Our group&#39;s calculator should keep up, because current calculators cannot solve this situation anymore. (xyIvneu_)</summary><p>?</p></details><details><summary>Calculating the theoretical limit with an oscillating acid environment is even more abstract; calculators cannot do it directly yet. If only variables could be entered. (Orirock)</summary><p>An oscillating acid environment can be represented using a “0-power recipe producing environment” and “recipes consuming environment.” One acid drop supplies 5s*4 simultaneous machines=20 environment units. Recipe duration equals the quantity of environment consumed. For lower utilization, change 4 machines to 3.</p><p>Solving for variables that cannot be expressed as linear constraints may be difficult; those that can be rewritten as linear constraints do not need variable notation.</p></details><details><summary>Calculators should support uploading production-line combinations. (xylvneu_)</summary><p>If the aim is to reduce a fixed-rate-ratio recipe chain to one recipe (with “production line” in its name), just write that recipe into the data file. Or is automatic reduction needed?</p></details></div>',1)])])}const h=t(o,[["render",r]]);export{m as __pageData,h as default};
